{
 "handle": "@CodingCulprit",
 "voice": "am_michael",
 "speed": 1.03,
 "lead": 0.35,
 "outro": 1.0,
 "gap": 0.26,
 "music": "bed",
 "title": "Consistent hashing",
 "sources": [
  "Wikipedia, 'Consistent hashing' (secondary source, fetched 2026-11-17): with hash-mod-n, a change in the number of slots causes nearly all keys to be remapped; consistent hashing maps both keys and servers onto a unit circle; each key goes to the next server clockwise; adding or removing a server moves only nearby keys; virtual nodes (duplicate labels per server) reduce skew from uneven placement; the term was introduced by David Karger et al. at MIT in 1997. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consistent_hashing",
  "Illustration, not from the source: three-then-four servers is an example, and a mass cache miss is the consequence for a cache."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "You spread cache keys over three servers with hash mod three, and it works fine, until you add a fourth.",
   "caps": "*3 SERVERS | HASH *MOD 3 | ADD A *FOURTH"
  },
  {
   "text": "Change the number of servers, and nearly all the keys get remapped to a different server.",
   "caps": "*NEARLY ALL KEYS | *MOVE"
  },
  {
   "text": "In a cache, that means almost everything misses at once, and your database takes the hit.",
   "caps": "*EVERYTHING MISSES | DATABASE *TAKES THE HIT"
  },
  {
   "text": "Consistent hashing fixes it by mapping both the servers and the keys onto a circle, called a hash ring.",
   "caps": "SERVERS *AND KEYS | ON A *CIRCLE | THE *HASH RING"
  },
  {
   "text": "Each key belongs to the next server clockwise from it.",
   "caps": "EACH KEY | NEXT SERVER *CLOCKWISE"
  },
  {
   "text": "Add a server, and only the keys right next to it move, a small share of the total.",
   "caps": "ADD A SERVER | ONLY *NEARBY KEYS MOVE"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: with only a few points on the ring, one server can end up with a huge arc, and an uneven load.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | FEW POINTS | *UNEVEN LOAD"
  },
  {
   "text": "So give each server many virtual nodes, spread around the ring, and the load evens out.",
   "caps": "*VIRTUAL NODES | SPREAD *AROUND THE RING | LOAD *EVENS OUT"
  }
 ],
